Fill an inactive tab with the opacity of the window

The fill of an inactive tab takes the opacity of the window or the
opacity of a dialog. The paint divides that value by one hundred and
fifty. The alpha therefore steps from one at the value one hundred to
two thirds at the value ninety nine. No value below one hundred gives a
solid tab.

The paint now divides the value by one hundred. The alpha follows the
spin box, so the tab fades with the window. The tab no longer takes more
translucency than the window. A value of one hundred keeps the solid
fill of before.

The change sits on the branch work/style-paint of the local tree, as commit 12 of 21.
A new merge request on system/qtcurve takes that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

diff --git a/qt6/style/qtcurve_api.cpp b/qt6/style/qtcurve_api.cpp
index 08a1ab93..8a7b7edf 100644
--- a/qt6/style/qtcurve_api.cpp
+++ b/qt6/style/qtcurve_api.cpp
@@ -3808,12 +3808,10 @@ Style::drawControl(ControlElement element, const QStyleOption *option,
                 QWidget *top=widget ? widget->window() : 0L;
                 bool isDialog = top && qtcIsDialog(top);
 
-                // Note: opacity is divided by 150 to make dark
-                // inactive tabs more translucent
                 if (isDialog && 100 != opts.dlgOpacity) {
-                    fill.setAlphaF(opts.dlgOpacity / 150.0);
+                    fill.setAlphaF(opts.dlgOpacity / 100.0);
                 } else if (!isDialog && 100 != opts.bgndOpacity) {
-                    fill.setAlphaF(opts.bgndOpacity / 150.0);
+                    fill.setAlphaF(opts.bgndOpacity / 100.0);
                 }
             }
 
